/test_the_sidebar.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_sources_page.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_theme_page_shows_its_art.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_theme_screen.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_themes_index.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_walls.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_walls_lead_with_the_work.py", "arrt/tests/contract/test_client_surface.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_client_tools.py", "arrt/tests/integration/test_theme_routes.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_displays.py", "tests/preferences/test_screen_tables.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ui-pass-themes-players.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/ia-proposal.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/information-architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/change-log.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: three Chunk 04 deliverables cited by short paths the lint cannot resolve", "recommendation": "record_lint reports `core/hanging.js` (plan line 254), `persistence/migrations.py` (257) and `screens/walls.js` (273) as declared deliverables that do not exist. All three files are in the tree, at arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/hanging.js, arrt/src/arrt/persistence/migrations.py and arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js. The lint fires because Chunk 04's Surfaces cites them by shortened paths, while the same chunk writes `arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/theme.js` in full, and that one resolves. Scope: instance. Spell the three references repo-relative in the plan's Chunk 04 Surfaces. migrations.py is context there, not a deliverable, so it can lose its code formatting instead. No code change is needed.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ui-pass-themes-players.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "A theme's Edit\u2026 sends a description no browser test drives, including the rename that must keep it", "recommendation": "Chunk 04 Done-when 1 says the browser suite 'edits the name and the description ... each read back from the server with a non-default value'. No browser test fills Edit\u2026's Description field or reads back a theme's description; grep finds 'Description' in the browser suite only in test_the_themes_index.py's New theme\u2026 test. Every rename test in TestRenaming uses a theme with no description. theme.js editOpener now posts `{name, description: description.value.trim() || null}` on every Save, so a rename keeps the description only because the textarea is pre-filled from `theme.description`. That is the hazard EditTheme's own docstring names ('would erase a description every time a curator corrected a typo in a name'). Drop the pre-fill and every test stays green; the route-level integration tests cannot see this, because the client is what sends the key. Per the learnings rule 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value', add browser tests (a) that set a description through Edit\u2026 and read it back from services.display.get_theme, and see it in `.theme-description`, (b) that rename a theme that has a description and assert the description survives on the server, and (c) that clear it to whitespace and get None. Watch each one fail on a re-break. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/theme.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_theme_screen.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Edit\u2026 and Delete\u2026 on a theme: leaving them is not asserted from the server", "recommendation": "Chunk 04 Done-when 4 says 'Cancel or Escape in each dialog changes nothing, asserted from the server'. Hang\u2026 does this (test_leaving_hang_as_it_was_or_closing_it_sends_nothing). Edit\u2026 does not: the only tests that press Escape in it (test_the_controls_that_act_on_a_theme_say_which_theme and the default test at line 761) change no field and assert nothing afterwards. Declining Delete\u2026 (test_declining_leaves_the_theme_where_it_was) asserts only that the h1 is still painted, not that the theme is still in the catalogue. Consequence: an Edit\u2026 whose onClose saved what was typed, or a Delete whose decline still sent DELETE before the repaint, would pass. Add one parametrised test (Cancel, Escape) that types a new name and description into Edit\u2026, leaves the dialog, and asserts the server's theme is unchanged. Have the Delete decline test also assert services.display.get_theme still returns the theme. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_the_theme_screen.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Retiring Clients' assign tests left the taken-screen case without a browser test", "recommendation": "test_an_output_already_showing_a_wall_is_named_and_a_second_is_refused_beside_assign was removed with assigning from Clients (ruling 7). It held two things: the picker named the wall a screen already had ('has {wall}'), and the server's 'already shows' refusal was said beside the act with nothing changed. Assigning now happens only in Walls' Edit\u2026 whereSection, whose Screen picker names each screen by screenName and connection alone. So a curator is offered a screen that already shows another wall, with no sign of it until the refusal. No browser test in test_editing_a_wall.py or test_the_walls.py drives that refusal; it is pinned only at unit/MCP level (test_clients.py:275, test_client_tools.py:214). Neither the plan's decisions nor the change-log names this test's successor. Add a test_editing_a_wall.py case: put a second wall on a screen that already shows one, assert the refusal with ui.said_beside on 'Show {wall} there', and assert the placement is unchanged on the server. Then either name the wall in the option, as Clients did, or record that the picker no longer says it. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_clients.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Samsung Frame naming is tested on Players only, not in Walls' screen picker", "recommendation": "The Chunk 02 decision says screenName names a Frame 'Samsung Frame' on Players and in Walls' screen picker alike. test_displays_and_labels.py:98 pins it on Players. The Walls caller (walls.js:1484, `screenName(output.name, output.kind)`) is reached only by test_editing_a_wall.py, which picks the Frame by value 'frame' and never reads the option's text. So the `output.kind` argument on that caller can be dropped and every test stays green. A cheap fix: assert the option's label in the existing Frame test. Scope: instance.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "core/hanging.js now holds two hang paths with two different questions for one act, and its header still says the module exists to prevent exactly that", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Chunk 04 added `hangOnWalls` (used only by screens/theme.js) next to `hangTheme` (used only by screens/walls.js). Each has its own preview fetch, its own question and its own `/activate` call. For the same act, hanging theme X on one wall Y, Walls asks `Hang X on Y?` with `<summary> Everyone in the house sees Y change.` and the Theme page asks the same title with `Y: <summary> Everyone in the house sees Y change.`. The module header says it is in `core/` because \"two screens perform the identical act\", and that \"A change made to the question in one screen and not the other ships two different questions for one act\". The module now ships that defect itself, and the header tells a maintainer the two screens share one question when they do not. The header is also stale on other points: it says the Theme screen offers \"Hang on the living room\" beside a theme and that it \"leaves for the Walls screen to show the result\". It now has *Hang\u2026* with checkboxes, and it repaints in place. Fix: build the question in one place, for example make `hangTheme` a thin call to `hangOnWalls({hangOn: [wall], takeDownFrom: []})`, or have both use one shared question builder. Then rewrite the header to say what is shared now. Do not add a note that the two must match.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/hanging.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/theme.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "A third private copy of the \"a, b and c\" list-joiner", "recommendation": "Scope: class. `hangOnWalls` defines an inline `listed`. It matches `listed` in screens/clients.js, the inline join in screens/work.js `wallConsequence`, and the parts form `listedParts` in screens/walls.js. Search the static tree for `slice(0, -1)` and `\" and \"` joins. Fix it by construction: move one `listed`/`listedParts` pair into a `core/` module and have every copy import it. The point is that a wording change (an Oxford comma, a locale) happens once.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/hanging.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/clients.js"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "screenName names every frame-kind output \"Samsung Frame\" and drops the Player's own name for it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `screenName(outputName, kind)` returns \"Samsung Frame\" for any `kind === \"frame\"`, whatever the output is called. The contract (`client-heartbeat.v1` schema) allows `kind: frame` on any uniquely named output, so a Player reporting two Frames would show two identical options in a wall's Edit\u2026 screen picker and two identical lines in its Players row. The curator could then hang a wall on the wrong television. That contradicts the function's own rule that a port with no person's name \"is shown as the Player names it, never guessed at\". Per the learning \"When you argue a derived artifact is device-independent, name a second, different device\": keep \"Samsung Frame\" when the output is the single `frame`, and otherwise add the reported name, for example `Samsung Frame (frame-2)`.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/outputs.js"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "The theme-edit route still has its rename-only name, and EditTheme's docstring narrates its history", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `EditTheme` was renamed \"for what it now carries\", but the handler that takes it is still `rename_theme`, and FastAPI derives the OpenAPI operationId from that name. Rename the handler to match, or record why the operationId is kept. Separately, delete the history clause from `EditTheme`'s docstring (\"which is why the body carried a name alone until a theme's *Edit\u2026* needed both\"). The sentinel reasoning before it is the part a maintainer needs. Wording only: nothing reads either of these.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/api.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/models.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Rename survivor: .env.example still sends the operator to Settings \u203a Clients to assign a wall's Player", "recommendation": "Learnings cross-check, escalated. core.md: 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording...'. This branch renames Settings \u203a Clients to Settings \u203a Players and moves assigning a wall off that page into the wall's Edit\u2026 dialog. .env.example was not touched and still says, at line 50, that which Player shows which wall 'is assigned on Settings \u203a Clients', and at lines 56-58 that 'The server issues each client's token on Settings \u203a Clients, and which Player shows which wall ... is assigned there too.' Both are now false: no page has that label, and the page under that address no longer assigns. An operator setting up a second wall from .env.example looks for a page and a control that are gone. This is the actively-misleading instruction Goal 4 rates BLOCKING, and it repeats the pattern the rule warns against. Scope: class. A whole-repo grep for 'Settings \u203a Clients' outside history (change-log, archived plans, operator-verification's older entries) finds only these two .env.example sites, so fix both: the token is on Settings \u203a Players, and a wall's Player is set in that wall's Edit\u2026 on Walls. A `Retires:` line naming 'Settings \u203a Clients' in this scope's change-log fragment would let record_lint's `retired-claim-survives` check catch the next survivor; this one got past because the fragment has no such line.", "files": [".env.example"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "A known Walls defect the plan flags as unfixed has no durable record beyond the plan", "recommendation": "The plan's mid-build decision 02 records a real defect: on Walls, a wall's Edit\u2026 opened in the moment before the page repaints after another dialog's write offers what was true before that write (a panel just stopped is not offered). It says the fix (each dialog reading a fresh listing as it opens) 'is Walls' and is for the backlog'. The test that found it was changed to wait for the repaint. No open item records it: cache-query searches for repaint, 'fresh listing', dialog and Edit\u2026 find nothing matching, and affecting screens/walls.js returns 0 items. When the plan is archived at merge, the only record of the defect goes with it, and the test no longer exercises the window. Record the defect somewhere that outlives the plan (cite it from the decision line), or fix it. Scope: instance.", "files": [".pra